{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":896,"output":2248,"cacheRead":3136,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1403,"totalTokens":6280,"cost":{"input":0.00040320000000000004,"output":0.0071936000000000005,"cacheRead":0.00015680000000000002,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.007753600000000001}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Alright, let me implement it. Requirements:\n\nValidation:\n- requests: must be an array\n- Each request: id is a non-empty string, unique across requests; desk is present (string?); requested is a positive finite number; priority is an integer; submittedAt is a valid timestamp.\n- capacityByDesk: an object, with a finite non-negative capacity for every desk referenced by a request.\n- Invalid input should throw a TypeError, without mutating either input (in any case we don't mutate the input).\n\nAllocation: per desk, sort by priority descending, submittedAt ascending (Date parse), id ascending (lexicographic). Allocate greedily.\n\nReturn rows in the original order, as { id, allocated, unfilled }.\n\nNo mutation of input.\n\nEdge cases: requested is positive finite — could it be a float? \"positive finite requested\" — not necessarily an integer. Allocation amount: at most the requested amount. Capacity is finite and non-negative.\n\nWriting the implementation:\n\n```js\nfunction validateInputs(requests, capacityByDesk) {\n  if (!Array.isArray(requests)) throw new TypeError(\"requests must be an array\");\n  if (!capacityByDesk || typeof capacityByDesk !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(capacityByDesk)) {\n    throw new TypeError(\"capacityByDesk must be an object\");\n  }\n\n  const seenIds = new Set();\n  const desks = new Set();\n\n  for (const request of requests) {\n    if (!request || typeof request !== \"object\" || Array.isArray(request)) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"each request must be an object\");\n    }\n    if (typeof request.id !== \"string\" || request.id.length === 0) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"request.id must be a non-empty string\");\n    }\n    if (seenIds.has(request.id)) throw new TypeError(`duplicate request id: ${request.id}`);\n    seenIds.add(request.id);\n    if (typeof request.desk !== \"string\" || request.desk.length === 0) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"request.desk must be a non-empty string\");\n    }\n    desks.add(request.desk);\n    if (typeof request.requested !== \"number\" || !Number.isFinite(request.requested) || request.requested <= 0) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"request.requested must be a positive finite number\");\n    }\n    if (!Number.isInteger(request.priority)) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"request.priority must be an integer\");\n    }\n    const submitted = Date.parse(request.submittedAt);\n    if (typeof request.submittedAt !== \"string\" || Number.isNaN(submitted)) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"request.submittedAt must be a valid timestamp\");\n    }\n  }\n\n  for (const desk of desks) {\n    const capacity = capacityByDesk[desk];\n    if (typeof capacity !== \"number\" || !Number.isFinite(capacity) || capacity < 0) {\n      throw new TypeError(`capacityByDesk[${desk}] must be a finite non-negative number`);\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNote: Date.parse can handle booleans and such too. First check that it's a string. submittedAt is a \"valid submittedAt timestamp\" — probably a string. Use Number.isFinite on Date.parse.\n\nAllocation:\n\n```js\nexport function allocateCapacity(requests, capacityByDesk) {\n  validateInputs(requests, capacityByDesk);\n\n  const allocation = new Map(); // id -> allocated\n  for (const request of requests) allocation.set(request.id, 0);\n\n  const byDesk = new Map();\n  for (const request of requests) {\n    const list = byDesk.get(request.desk) ?? [];\n    list.push(request);\n    byDesk.set(request.desk, list);\n  }\n\n  for (const [desk, deskRequests] of byDesk) {\n    const ordered = [...deskRequests].sort((x, y) =>\n      y.priority - x.priority ||\n      Date.parse(x.submittedAt) - Date.parse(y.submittedAt) ||\n      x.id.localeCompare(y.id));\n\n    let remaining = capacityByDesk[desk];\n    for (const request of ordered) {\n      const granted = Math.min(request.requested, remaining);\n      allocation.set(request.id, granted);\n      remaining -= granted;\n    }\n  }\n\n  return requests.map((request) => ({\n    id: request.id,\n    allocated: allocation.get(request.id),\n    unfilled: request.requested - allocation.get(request.id),\n  }));\n}\n```\n\nWait — lexicographic comparison: should it be localeCompare or simple < comparison? \"Smaller id in lexicographic order\" — plain code-unit comparison is safer/deterministic: x.id < y.id ? -1 : x.id > y.id ? 1 : 0. localeCompare can be locale-dependent. Use plain comparison.\n\nNote: if there are duplicate ids, it would break the map, but validation prevents that.\n\nTests (at least 3):\n1. Per-desk independent allocation with priority ordering (same as committed test, or different).\n2. Tiebreaking by submittedAt, then by id.\n3. Validation: duplicate ids throw TypeError and input is not mutated; invalid timestamp; negative/missing capacity; non-integer priority.\n4. No mutation: check that input is not mutated via deep equality snapshots.\n5. Zero capacity: allocated is 0, unfilled is the requested amount.\n6. Result is in the original input order even when ordering differs.\n\nWriting the test file:\n\n```js\nimport assert from \"node:assert/strict\";\nimport test from \"node:test\";\n\nimport { allocateCapacity } from \"./allocation-engine.mjs\";\n\nconst validRequest = (overrides = {}) => ({\n  id: \"a\",\n  desk: \"ABS\",\n  requested: 10,\n  priority: 1,\n  submittedAt: \"2026-08-23T10:00:00Z\",\n  ...overrides,\n});\n\ntest(\"allocates per desk by priority, submission time, and id\", ...)\ntest(\"validates and throws TypeError without mutating inputs\", ...)\ntest(\"zero capacity leaves everything unfilled\", ...)\ntest(\"does not mutate inputs and preserves original row order\", ...)\n```\n\nMutation check: deep-copy the input, then assert.deepEqual after the call.\n\nI'll write both files.\n"}}
