ed, including the committed tests. Confirmed that only the two allowed files were changed — yes, only those were written.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"\n\nAll 8 tests pass (3 committed + 5 student tests).\n\n## Implementation approach\n\n**`allocation-engine.mjs`**\n- **Validation first, no side effects**: `validateInputs` checks the complete input before any allocation — `requests` is an array; each request is an object with a non-empty unique `id`, non-empty `desk`, positive finite `requested`, integer `priority`, and a timestamp string where `Date.parse` yields a finite number; and every referenced desk has a finite non-negative numeric capacity in `capacityByDesk`. Any violation throws `TypeError` before state is built, so inputs can never be touched.\n- **Per-desk greedy allocation**: requests are grouped by desk (copies in new maps/arrays — never the originals). Each desk's group is sorted by priority descending, then `submittedAt` ascending, then `id` by plain code-unit comparison (locale-independent lexicographic). The desk's capacity is granted greedily via `Math.min(requested, remaining)`.\n- **Output**: results are rebuilt from the original `requests` array order as `{ id, allocated, unfilled }`, with `unfilled = requested − allocated`. Inputs are only read, never mutated.\n\n## Edge cases covered by my tests\n\n1. **Ordering cascade**: a priority-2 tie resolved by earlier `submittedAt`, then by smaller `id` (b before y), while a lower-priority earlier request (`z`) still loses — plus independence across two desks.\n2. **Original row order + non-mutation**: result rows follow input order even though allocation order differed; deep-equality snapshots verify neither `requests` nor `capacityByDesk` are mutated.\n3. **Validation matrix** (9 cases, each asserting `TypeError` and unmutated inputs): duplicate id, empty id, zero and infinite `requested`, non-integer priority, invalid timestamp, missing desk capacity, negative capacit